David, who wrote it, knew sheep. Before he was a king he was a shepherd boy, and he understood how vulnerable sheep are. They wander. They panic. They cannot find water or safe pasture on their own. When David says "Yahweh is my shepherd," he is not flattering himself. He is admitting that he, too, needs leading.

Notice the verbs: he makes me lie down, he leads me, he restores my soul, he guides me. The psalm is full of things God does. Many of us live as though everything depends on our own effort, and we are exhausted by it. This psalm offers a different picture. Rest is not something we must earn; sometimes it is something we are gently made to do.

"He restores my soul" is a beautiful phrase. The Hebrew word carries the sense of bringing back, of returning something to where it belongs. Perhaps you feel scattered today, pulled in many directions. The shepherd''s work is to gather you back.

Then the psalm turns. "Even though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death." The green pastures do not last forever, and David does not pretend they do. Valleys come. What changes in the valley is the way David speaks. Up to this point he has spoken about God: "he leads me." In the valley he speaks to God: "you are with me." It is often in the darkest places that faith becomes most personal.

The rod and staff were a shepherd''s tools — one for protection, one for guidance and rescue. David finds them comforting, not threatening. God''s presence in the valley is not passive. He is there to protect and to bring you through.

Whatever season you are in today — rest or valley — this psalm invites you to know that you are not walking alone.', 'Take five unhurried minutes today to sit quietly with no screen. Let that pause be a small act of trust that you are being shepherded.', 'Are you in a season of green pastures or a valley right now? What would it look like to speak to God, rather than only about him, in that place?', 'Good Shepherd, I confess that I often try to lead myself. Thank you that you restore my soul and walk with me even in the valley. Help me to rest in your care today, and to trust you with the path ahead. Amen.', array['finding_peace','anxiety','strengthening_faith']::text[], false, 0) on conflict (slug) do update set title = excluded.title, book_id = excluded.book_id, chapter = excluded.chapter, verse_start = excluded.verse_start, verse_end = excluded.verse_end, reflection = excluded.reflection, takeaway = excluded.takeaway, reflection_question = excluded.reflection_question, prayer = excluded.prayer, focus_tags = excluded.focus_tags, is_premium = excluded.is_premium, sort_order = excluded.sort_order;

It helps to remember where Paul was when he wrote this letter. He was in chains, uncertain whether he would live or die. He was not writing from a comfortable study. His encouragement comes from someone who knew real reasons to be afraid.

Paul does not say, ‘Stop worrying and think about something else.’ He offers a practice: "in everything, by prayer and petition with thanksgiving, let your requests be made known to God." Worry tends to circle inside us, repeating itself. Prayer gives it somewhere to go. Each anxious thought can become a request — named honestly, specifically, without needing the right words.

The phrase "with thanksgiving" is important. Thankfulness does not deny the problem; it places it in a wider frame. When we remember what God has already done, the problem in front of us takes its proper size. Many people find it helpful to name three specific things they are grateful for before they begin asking.

The promise that follows is not that every problem will be solved the way we hope. It is that "the peace of God, which surpasses all understanding, will guard your hearts and your thoughts." The word ‘guard’ is a military term, picturing a garrison standing watch. God''s peace stands guard over the very places — the heart and the mind — where anxiety tends to attack.

This peace "surpasses all understanding." Sometimes it arrives before the situation makes any sense. People who have walked through illness, grief or uncertainty often describe a calm they cannot explain. That is the kind of peace Paul is describing.

It is worth saying clearly: anxiety is not a spiritual failure. If worry is overwhelming you or affecting daily life, talking to your GP or a counsellor is a wise and good step, and many Christians find that prayer and professional support work well together. Today''s invitation is simply to bring what is on your mind to God, one request at a time.', 'Write down the one worry that is loudest today. Then turn it into a simple, one-sentence prayer and add one thing you are thankful for.', 'What is one worry you have been carrying alone? What would it feel like to name it honestly to God?', 'Lord, you know the thoughts that keep circling in my mind. I bring them to you now, one by one. Thank you for your faithfulness to me so far. Guard my heart and my thoughts with your peace, even before I understand how things will work out. Amen.', array['anxiety','prayer','finding_peace']::text[], false, 1) on conflict (slug) do update set title = excluded.title, book_id = excluded.book_id, chapter = excluded.chapter, verse_start = excluded.verse_start, verse_end = excluded.verse_end, reflection = excluded.reflection, takeaway = excluded.takeaway, reflection_question = excluded.reflection_question, prayer = excluded.prayer, focus_tags = excluded.focus_tags, is_premium = excluded.is_premium, sort_order = excluded.sort_order;

That is precisely why the verses in today''s passage shine so brightly. They do not come from someone whose life is going well. They come from the middle of the ruins.

Notice how the passage begins: "Remember my affliction and my misery." The writer is honest about his pain. He even says his soul is "bowed down" within him. Faith in the Bible is rarely tidy. The Psalms and Lamentations give us permission to bring raw grief to God rather than hiding it behind polite words.

Then comes a turning point: "This I recall to my mind; therefore I have hope." Hope does not arrive because circumstances have changed. Nothing about the ruined city is different. What changes is what the writer chooses to remember. He deliberately calls to mind God''s loving kindness and compassion.

"They are new every morning." This is a gentle and practical truth. Yesterday''s strength may have run out. Yesterday''s mistakes may still sting. But God''s mercy does not run on yesterday''s supply. Each morning it is offered fresh.

"Yahweh is my portion," the writer says. In ancient Israel, land was divided into portions for each family, but the priests received no land — God himself was their portion. To say God is your portion is to say that even when other things are taken away, you have not lost everything.

If you are grieving today, you do not need to rush past your sorrow. Lamentations shows us that lament and hope can sit side by side. You can say, ‘This hurts,’ and also, ‘Great is your faithfulness.’ Both are honest. Both are prayer.', 'Tomorrow morning, before you check your phone, say one sentence to God: "Your mercies are new this morning." Notice how it shapes the start of your day.', 'What do you need to "call to mind" today in order to hold on to hope?', 'Faithful God, some days I feel worn down and hope feels far away. Thank you that your mercies do not run out, and that this morning they are new. Be my portion when other things fall short. I choose to remember your faithfulness today. Amen.', array['grief','finding_peace','strengthening_faith']::text[], false, 2) on conflict (slug) do update set title = excluded.title, book_id = excluded.book_id, chapter = excluded.chapter, verse_start = excluded.verse_start, verse_end = excluded.verse_end, reflection = excluded.reflection, takeaway = excluded.takeaway, reflection_question = excluded.reflection_question, prayer = excluded.prayer, focus_tags = excluded.focus_tags, is_premium = excluded.is_premium, sort_order = excluded.sort_order;

Notice who is invited. Not the strong, the organised or the spiritually accomplished, but the tired. The word ‘labour’ suggests exhausting work, and "heavily burdened" pictures someone carrying a load that is too heavy. Jesus'' first listeners were often poor, working long hours, and living under a religious system that could feel like an endless list of rules. Many of us carry different burdens today — caring for others, financial pressure, the quiet weight of expectations — but the invitation is the same.

Jesus does not begin by asking us to try harder. He begins with "come." Before any instruction, there is relationship.

Then he says something surprising: "Take my yoke upon you." A yoke was a wooden beam fitted across the necks of oxen so they could pull a load together. At first it sounds like more work. But Jesus'' point is that he offers a different yoke from the one we are already wearing. Rabbis of the time spoke of taking on ‘the yoke’ of their teaching. Jesus offers his own way of life, and he describes it as ‘easy’ and ‘light’.

The word translated "easy" can also mean ‘well-fitting’ or ‘kind’. Carpenters made yokes carefully so that they would not rub or injure the animal. Jesus is not offering a life without effort, but a life where the load fits, where we are not crushed by burdens we were never meant to carry alone.

"learn from me, for I am gentle and humble in heart." This is one of the few times Jesus describes his own character, and he chooses the words gentle and humble. He is not a harsh master waiting for us to fail.

Rest, in this passage, is not only a nap or a holiday, valuable as those are. It is "rest for your souls" — a deep settledness that comes from walking with someone who is gentle, and who shares the weight.', 'Identify one burden that you have been carrying as though it all depends on you. Name it to Jesus today and ask what it would mean to share that yoke with him.', 'Which burdens in your life feel like they were never meant to be carried alone?', 'Jesus, I hear your invitation to come. I am tired in ways I do not always admit. I bring you the weight I have been carrying. Teach me your gentle and humble way, and give my soul rest. Amen.', array['finding_peace','building_habits','strengthening_faith']::text[], false, 3) on conflict (slug) do update set title = excluded.title, book_id = excluded.book_id, chapter = excluded.chapter, verse_start = excluded.verse_start, verse_end = excluded.verse_end, reflection = excluded.reflection, takeaway = excluded.takeaway, reflection_question = excluded.reflection_question, prayer = excluded.prayer, focus_tags = excluded.focus_tags, is_premium = excluded.is_premium, sort_order = excluded.sort_order;

His answer begins with a question: "If God is for us, who can be against us?" Paul is not claiming that Christians face no opposition — he knew persecution, hunger and danger personally. His point is that no opposition can ultimately overturn God''s purposes for those he loves.

He grounds this confidence in the cross. "He who didn''t spare his own Son, but delivered him up for us all, how would he not also with him freely give us all things?" If God has already given the greatest gift, we can trust his heart in smaller matters too.

Then Paul lists hardships: trouble, anguish, persecution, famine, nakedness, danger and the sword. These were not abstract ideas for the first readers in Rome. Within a few years, many of them would face terrible persecution under the emperor Nero. Paul does not promise to remove these things. Instead he says that "in all these things, we are more than conquerors through him who loved us." Notice the phrase "in all these things" — not by avoiding them, but in the middle of them.

The final two verses sweep across everything Paul can imagine: death and life, angels and rulers, things present and things to come, height and depth, and "any other created thing." Nothing in all creation, he concludes, "will be able to separate us from God''s love, which is in Christ Jesus our Lord."

Christians from different traditions understand assurance in slightly different ways, but this passage has comforted believers across the centuries. If you sometimes fear that your doubts, failures or circumstances might cut you off from God, Paul''s words are worth reading aloud. The love he describes is not fragile. It holds.', 'Read verses 38–39 aloud slowly. Then add your own specific fear to Paul''s list and say, "Even this cannot separate me from God''s love."', 'What fear or circumstance has made you feel distant from God''s love lately?', 'Loving God, thank you that your love is stronger than anything I fear. When I feel distant, remind me that nothing in all creation can separate me from you. Help me to live today with the confidence of someone who is held. Amen.', array['strengthening_faith','anxiety','finding_purpose']::text[], false, 4) on conflict (slug) do update set title = excluded.title, book_id = excluded.book_id, chapter = excluded.chapter, verse_start = excluded.verse_start, verse_end = excluded.verse_end, reflection = excluded.reflection, takeaway = excluded.takeaway, reflection_question = excluded.reflection_question, prayer = excluded.prayer, focus_tags = excluded.focus_tags, is_premium = excluded.is_premium, sort_order = excluded.sort_order;

It is worth noticing the order. Before Paul says what to put on, he reminds his readers who they are: chosen, holy and beloved. Kindness is not a way of earning God''s love; it flows from already being loved. People who know they are loved tend to find it easier to be gentle with others.

Each quality Paul lists is relational. Compassion feels with others. Kindness acts for their good. Humility does not insist on being first. Gentleness — or "lowliness" — refuses to crush. Patience gives people time. None of these can be practised alone in a room; they are tested in families, workplaces, churches and friendships.

Then Paul becomes very realistic: "bearing with one another, and forgiving each other, if any man has a complaint against any." He assumes there will be complaints. Christian community has never been a gathering of people who never annoy each other. It is a gathering of people learning to forgive.

The standard for forgiveness is striking: "even as Christ forgave you, so you also do." Forgiveness does not mean pretending something did not happen, or that harm did not matter, or that trust must be restored immediately. Where there has been abuse or danger, forgiveness never requires anyone to stay unsafe. But forgiveness does mean releasing the right to repay, and choosing over time not to let bitterness rule.

Over all these, Paul says, put on love, "which is the bond of perfection" — the belt that holds the whole outfit together. And then: "let the peace of God rule in your hearts." The word ‘rule’ is an athletic term for an umpire. Peace is meant to be the referee in our relationships, settling disputes inside us before they spill out.

Today, as you dress, you might pause and choose one of these qualities to ‘put on’ for the people you will meet.', 'Choose one quality from the passage — compassion, kindness, humility, gentleness or patience — and look for one specific chance to practise it today.', 'Who in your life is hardest to "bear with" right now? What might one small step of kindness toward them look like?', 'Father, thank you that I am chosen and loved by you. Clothe me today with compassion, kindness, humility, gentleness and patience. Where I am holding on to resentment, give me grace to begin to forgive as you have forgiven me. Let your peace rule in my heart. Amen.', array['relationships','forgiveness']::text[], false, 5) on conflict (slug) do update set title = excluded.title, book_id = excluded.book_id, chapter = excluded.chapter, verse_start = excluded.verse_start, verse_end = excluded.verse_end, reflection = excluded.reflection, takeaway = excluded.takeaway, reflection_question = excluded.reflection_question, prayer = excluded.prayer, focus_tags = excluded.focus_tags, is_premium = excluded.is_premium, sort_order = excluded.sort_order;

He describes "a thorn in the flesh." We do not know exactly what it was. Over the centuries people have suggested an illness, a problem with his eyesight, ongoing opposition, or a personal struggle. Perhaps it is good that we do not know, because it allows many of us to recognise our own thorn in his story.

Paul did what any of us would do: he prayed for it to be taken away. "Concerning this thing, I begged the Lord three times that it might depart from me." This is important. It is not unspiritual to ask God to remove pain. Paul asked earnestly and more than once.

The answer he received was not the one he wanted: "My grace is sufficient for you, for my power is made perfect in weakness." God did not remove the thorn, but he promised enough grace to live with it.

This can be hard to hear. Sometimes we long for a quick fix, and the Bible is honest that not every prayer is answered in the way we hope. It is worth being careful here: this passage does not mean that every struggle is sent by God, nor that we should refuse help, treatment or support. Paul himself relied on friends, including Luke, who was a doctor. Rather, it reminds us that God''s presence and strength are not limited to the times when everything is fixed.

Paul''s response is surprising: "Most gladly therefore I will rather glory in my weaknesses, that the power of Christ may rest on me." He discovered that weakness opened space for a strength that was not his own. When he could no longer rely on his own resources, he learned to rely on Christ.

"When I am weak, then am I strong." This is not a slogan for pretending everything is fine. It is an honest description of a life where our limits become meeting places with God''s grace.', 'Name your own "thorn" honestly in prayer. Ask God for what you need, and also ask for grace to meet you in the meantime.', 'Where do you feel weak right now? How might God''s grace meet you there, even if the situation does not change quickly?', 'Lord, you know the thorn I carry. I ask you honestly to help me with it. And while I wait, let your grace be enough for today. Where I am weak, let your strength rest on me. Amen.', array['strengthening_faith','finding_purpose','anxiety']::text[], false, 6) on conflict (slug) do update set title = excluded.title, book_id = excluded.book_id, chapter = excluded.chapter, verse_start = excluded.verse_start, verse_end = excluded.verse_end, reflection = excluded.reflection, takeaway = excluded.takeaway, reflection_question = excluded.reflection_question, prayer = excluded.prayer, focus_tags = excluded.focus_tags, is_premium = excluded.is_premium, sort_order = excluded.sort_order;
delete from public.quiz_questions where question in ('Who built an ark to survive the flood?', 'In which town was Jesus born?', 'How many disciples did Jesus choose to be the Twelve?', 'What did David use to defeat Goliath?', 'Which book comes first in the New Testament?', 'What did Jesus feed five thousand people with?', 'Who was swallowed by a great fish?', 'According to Genesis 1, what did God create on the first day?', 'Which apostle denied knowing Jesus three times?', 'On the road to which city was Saul (Paul) converted?', 'Which psalm begins with the words that Yahweh is "my shepherd"?', 'In Paul''s letter, which three things "remain"?', 'Which river was Jesus baptised in?', 'In the parable, who stopped to help the man beaten by robbers?', 'How many days and nights did Jesus fast in the wilderness?', 'Which prophet was taken up to heaven in a whirlwind?', 'Who was the first king of Israel?', 'Which book of the Bible does not mention God by name?', 'What was the name of the garden where Jesus prayed before his arrest (as named by Matthew and Mark)?', 'Who wrote down the vision recorded in Revelation, while on the island of Patmos?', 'I led the Israelites out of Egypt and received the Ten Commandments on Mount Sinai. Who am I?', 'I was thrown into a den of lions but God shut their mouths. Who am I?', 'I was sold by my brothers and later became a ruler in Egypt. Who am I?', 'I said to my mother-in-law, "Where you go, I will go." Who am I?', 'I was a tax collector who climbed a sycamore tree to see Jesus. Who am I?', 'I came to Jesus at night and he told me I must be born anew. Who am I?', 'I said I would not believe unless I saw the nail marks in Jesus'' hands. Who am I?', 'I prayed for a son at the tabernacle and dedicated Samuel to the Lord. Who am I?', 'I was a seller of purple cloth whose heart the Lord opened as Paul spoke in Philippi. Who am I?', 'I rebuilt the walls of Jerusalem after serving as cupbearer to the Persian king. Who am I?');
